WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:08.680
REST &amp; VESTERS:
That was a Twitter tech lead admitting that

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.549
he and most of his colleagues only work 4
hours every week.

00:00:13.549 --> 00:00:20.240
Despite this terrible performance, this guy
was likely paid around $341,000 every single

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.240
year.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.609
Put another way, he was basically getting
paid over $1,600 per hour.

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:30.869
Clearly, Elon put an end to this at Twitter
but this is still very much a reality within

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:31.869
most tech companies.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:36.250
Now, I don’t want to throw all engineers
under the bus as a substantial portion of

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:41.100
them are the exact opposite, working 60 to
80 hours every week.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.829
But with that being said, there is a signifcant
number of engineers just like this.

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:52.030
In fact, this “community” is so large
that there’s a name for people like this

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:53.399
“rest and vesters”.

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:57.620
They just rest and vest their insanely high
compensation packages.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.960
You would think that managers hate these type
of people and that they’re constantly looking

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:06.520
to expose and fire these freeloaders but that’s
not actually the case.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.470
In fact, a lot of managers do this themselves
and they sometimes even encourage their team

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:12.990
to do it as well.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.110
This one high level engineer at Facebook for
example was making a million per year and

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.750
leading about 36 people.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:23.360
While the pay and prestige was great, she
was getting pretty burnt out with her job,

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.579
so she did the respectable thing and told
her boss who was likely a senior director

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:30.430
that she was going to leave in about 6 months.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.640
She was willing to finish up all the projects
that she had already taken on but didn’t

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:35.720
want anymore.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.320
To this, her boss told her to not come to
work the next day.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:45.070
In any other industry or even a legacy tech
company, this means: You’re fired, don’t

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.549
bother showing up tomorrow.

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:49.820
At Facebook though this meant "Just don’t
come to work.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.780
You're burned out and need a break.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.110
Just don’t talk about it and everyone will
assume you're on someone else’s team."

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:00.469
Clearly, this is a not a group of rebels that
are pushing the boundaries.

00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:03.439
This is a systematic trend at these companies.

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:08.340
Many companies are cracking down on this with
layoffs more recently, but for the most part,

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.879
they just turn a blind eye to the entire situation.

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:16.569
So, here’s why FAANG engineers are allowed
to work so little while getting paid so much.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:22.160
FRIENDLY ECONOMICS:
One of the biggest factors that makes resting

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.860
and vesting possible is simply the economics
of these companies.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.990
Unlike a factory or a bottling plant, these
tech companies have insanely high gross margins.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.170
Apple 43%.

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:34.610
Google 55%.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.349
Nvidia 56%.

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:38.030
And Facebook 78%.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:44.349
So, really, their only major expense is human
labor as their cost of goods sold is virtually

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:45.349
zero.

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:49.120
In fact, at Visa, the gross margin is straight
up 100%.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.110
As for the tech companies that don’t have
high gross margins yet, well they usually

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:57.209
just have infinite VC funding so they end
up in the same boat.

00:02:57.209 --> 00:03:01.760
With that being said, the top priority for
leaders of these companies is to simply reach

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.700
as many people as possible as quickly as possible,
to maximize growth.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:11.620
Efficiency is not something that they have
ever really prioritized until recently and

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:13.370
it’s no wonder why.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.600
Focusing on growth is simply way more beneficial.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.600
Here’s the thing, these companies are usually
able grow 30 to 50% per year for multiple

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.090
decades straight.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:26.650
Amazon for example hasn’t even had a single
quarter of negative revenue growth since the

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.140
beginning of this chart.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.680
So, it makes no sense to focus on the 20 or
30% of employees that are leeches or worse

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.900
get the people who are working hard to waste
time weeding out these freeloaders when leaders

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:41.670
can just focus on growing the entire company
by 50%.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.970
But all of that is just half the puzzle.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:47.680
The other half is that the employees also
enjoy super friendly economics.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.409
If an engineer is living paycheck to paycheck
and struggling to make rent, you can bet that

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:54.629
they’re gonna work hard to keep their job.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:57.610
But, this is rarely the case with engineers.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:01.080
If anything, they’re struggling to keep
up with their upscaled silicon valley life

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.520
with their $2 million mortgage and tesla model
s plaid.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.980
Realistically though, these engineers have
absolutely no problem paying for a base standard

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:12.319
of living especially with remote work becoming
more popular.

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:17.680
In other words, they’re not exactly dependent
on their jobs so they’re not exactly scared

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:18.680
of getting fired.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.350
In fact, they often don’t even care for
their job.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:25.669
They’re not looking for a promotion or to
climb to ladder or to do something impactful.

00:04:25.669 --> 00:04:29.930
The only reason they’re still working at
the company is to cash in on the stock compensation

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.240
that unlocks in 12 or 24 months and then hopefully
score another large package right after that.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:38.930
If they don’t get the package they’re
looking for, they’ll just jump ship to another

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:41.030
company who will pay them what they want.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:44.470
Now, you might be saying, who would hire such
a bum.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:48.760
Well, the number one factor that gets you
a job at these companies is how well you interview

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.710
and how well you can solve coding problems.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.730
And guess what, these guys are stars at this.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:57.280
That’s how they got their original job itself.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.720
They know what to do, they just choose not
to do it.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.840
So, unless their previous manager gives them
just an absolutely awful referral, if they’re

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:10.060
even asked for a referral, these people are
able to just jump from job to job and coast

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.060
as needed.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:15.759
So, if you wanna fire them, go ahead, they’ll
just turn around and find better paying jobs

00:05:15.759 --> 00:05:17.289
anyway.

00:05:17.289 --> 00:05:22.280
NO NEED:
Aside from the financials working out, the

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:26.840
reality is that there’s often just not much
to do at many of these jobs to begin with.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:30.950
When someone hears that you work at Google,
they instantly assume that you must be working

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.970
on something super cool or at least something
familiar.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:39.069
Maybe you’re working on the YouTube algorithm
or Google ads or even Google Bard but only

00:05:39.069 --> 00:05:42.250
the elite of the elite get to be on these
teams.

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:46.300
Everyone else is just tasked with the monotonous
work of maintaining and upgrading their existing

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:47.300
systems.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:51.830
In other words, the average Googler isn’t
strategizing how Bard can overtake ChatGPT.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:58.680
Rather, they’re probably working on the
423rd iteration of a Google places API that

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.220
will likely never see the light of day along
with 5 other engineers.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:06.390
The purpose of this iteration is to reduce
request times from 0.75 of a second to 0.5

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.810
a second for Apple M1 devices.

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:13.430
The team probably optimized the code within
a matter of hours but now they’re waiting

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:15.780
days for approval from upper management.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.539
In the meantime, they’re just writing some
documentation and having useless meetings

00:06:19.539 --> 00:06:21.580
as they wait to go home everyday.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.570
That is unfortunately the scope of most engineers
in big tech.

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:29.660
Obviously not that exciting and not that different
from traditional jobs.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:34.730
As these companies grew, so did their inefficiencies
and bureaucracy and so many engineers are

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:36.509
just simply stuck between the wheels.

00:06:36.509 --> 00:06:41.130
Now, an active employee might ask to be placed
on more teams and take on more work.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:45.150
But, for a passive employee who’s willing
to do what they’re asked but nothing more,

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.580
there’s simply not much more to do than
20 hours of work per week which brings us

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.530
into our next type of rest and vester which
is the 10X engineer.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:57.610
10X engineers aren’t exactly trying to work
as little as possible and just coast.

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:01.009
In fact, they may be actively going for a
promotion.

00:07:01.009 --> 00:07:04.420
They just don’t need all that much time
to kill it at their job.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:07.610
When you think about it, this is really not
that surprising.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:11.060
Given that these companies are looking to
hire the best talent possible for whatever

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:15.199
price, it’s only natural that they attract
a substantial number of super stars.

00:07:15.199 --> 00:07:19.199
These people are simply able to achieve what’s
expected or even go above and beyond within

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:21.070
a matter of 20 hours a week.

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:25.960
And given that most tech companies value results
over hours worked, these people fit right

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:26.960
in.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.360
Netflix for example offers unlimited paid
time off.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:32.900
The reason being that they don’t care about
you clocking in everyday.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:37.460
They care about what value you provide and
these 10X engineers more than carry their

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:38.460
weight.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:43.060
So, despite working a low number of hours,
these engineers aren’t exactly resting.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:47.150
They’re simply super productive when they
do work and they likely have several major

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:51.580
commitments outside of their job like working
on a startup or running a YouTube channel.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:55.910
The bottomline is that unless you’re aggressively
trying to climb the corporate ladder, there’s

00:07:55.910 --> 00:08:01.590
simply no need to work any more whether you’re
a passive engineer or a 10X engineer.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:07.069
PART OF THE PLAN:
Much of what we discussed so far simply seems

00:08:07.069 --> 00:08:08.849
to be circumstantial.

00:08:08.849 --> 00:08:13.659
Tech companies just happen to have great economics
and inefficiencies and bureaucracy and all

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:14.659
of that.

00:08:14.659 --> 00:08:19.770
But really, the only reason that rest and
vesters exist is because these companies want

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:20.900
them to exist.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:26.160
In fact, not only do they want them to exist,
they were literally the ones who created them.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:31.490
You see, the history of the rest and vesters
dates back to the origins of these companies.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:32.830
The first 50 engineers.

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:34.940
The first 100 engineers.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.899
These guys were the OG rest and vesters.

00:08:37.899 --> 00:08:41.560
Hold on back up, how could these guys have
been resting and vesting when the company

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.459
was at it’s most critical state.

00:08:43.459 --> 00:08:47.209
Well, they weren’t atleast not for the first
5 or 10 years.

00:08:47.209 --> 00:08:51.770
Back then, they were actually busting their
butts scaling Google or Facebook or whatever

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:52.770
else.

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:55.630
But eventually, the company makes it to the
other side.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:59.960
They become profitable, they hire large teams,
all the groundwork has been laid.

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:04.649
In the meantime, the original workforce has
also become extraordinarily rich.

00:09:04.649 --> 00:09:09.890
Each of them probably only got like a 0.1%
stake in the company but that itself is worth

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:12.440
tens of millions if not hundreds of millions.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:14.920
So, where do these people go from here?

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:19.550
Well, the most logical step would be to just
retire but these guys aren’t usually the

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:20.690
retiring type.

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:24.700
They’re more likely to join another startup
at an executive level position or found their

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.390
own startup likely in an adjacent field.

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:31.130
And see, this is where the problem arises.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:34.560
Given that these guys have been around since
the beginning, they’re well aware of all

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:39.209
of the mistakes Google or Facebook made along
the way and how to avoid these if they did

00:09:39.209 --> 00:09:40.209
it again.

00:09:40.209 --> 00:09:45.459
This makes these individuals a massive asset
for startups but also a massive liability

00:09:45.459 --> 00:09:47.190
for Google and Facebook.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:51.670
For obvious reasons, these companies don’t
want their early employees to help out or

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:53.149
even create competitors.

00:09:53.149 --> 00:09:58.090
So, they go to these employees and tell them:
Hey Jeff, we know how hard you’ve worked

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:01.579
over the past 10 years and how instrumental
you’ve been for this company.

00:10:01.579 --> 00:10:07.700
So, we’d like to give you a $100 million
stock package and the title of senior VP.

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:11.260
Only caveat is that you have to stick around
for another 10 years to unlock the entire

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:16.710
package by which point that’ll actually
be worth a billion so you definitely wanna

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:17.710
stick around.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:22.880
In the meantime, of course do a good job as
SVP but you don’t have to work to the bone

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:26.510
or anything and that’s where it all begins.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.950
These companies are essentially bribing these
employees to not leave.

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:30.950
They don’t even care how effective they
are as employees.

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:34.830
As long as they don’t become effective employees
for other companies, that’s a win.

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:39.760
Originally, this strategy was only reserved
for the most elite employees but it has since

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.570
worked its way down the corporate ladder.

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:45.860
A senior manager at Google for example could
easily switch to being an extremely pivotal

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:48.430
director or even VP at a startup.

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:53.950
So, Google basically just hands them half
a million a year and tells them to chill out.

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:56.750
Likely the best evidence of this is Google’s
hiring strategy.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:01.560
Till recently, they didn’t even know what
team their hires would end up in because they

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.450
didn’t hire based on need.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:06.940
They would just post generic job listings
like Software Engineer.

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:11.132
It wasn’t until they test and verify your
skills that they even tried to find a team

00:11:11.132 --> 00:11:12.132
for you.

00:11:12.132 --> 00:11:16.110
With every hire, they’re basically like:
Oh shoot, this guys actually skilled.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.160
We can’t let him work somewhere else.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:20.459
Uh, let’s just find a team for him.

00:11:20.459 --> 00:11:25.170
And that’s why FAANG engineers work so little
but earn so much.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:28.500
It’s because it’s all part of the bigger
plan.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:32.200
ChatGPT has taken this to the next level with
$900,000 salaries.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:38.170
Check out this video to learn more.

